1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guangxu_Emperor '''Guangxu Emperor'''], personal name Zaitian (Manchu: Dzai-Tiyan), was the eleventh emperor of the Qing dynasty, and the ninth Qing emperor to rule over China. Hi
2 CONC s reign lasted from 1875 to 1908, but in practice he ruled, under Empress Dowager Cixi's influence, only from 1889 to 1898. He initiated the Hundred Days' Reform, but was abruptly stopped when the empress dowager launched a coup i
2 CONC n 1898, after which he was put under house arrest until his death. His regnal name, "Guangxu", means "glorious succession"
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Tsai-t'ien''' 載湉, Aug. 14, 1871–1908, Nov. 14, ninth Emperor of the Ch'ing Dynasty, was a grandson of [https://www.geni.com/people/Daoguang-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E9%81%93%E5%85%89%E5%B8%9D/6000000008737149905 Emperor 
2 CONC Hsüan-tsung] (Min-ning, q.v.), a nephew of [https://www.geni.com/people/Xianfeng-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%92%B8%E8%B1%90%E5%B8%9D/6000000008737283555 Emperor Wên-tsung] (I-chu, q.v.), and a cousin of [https://www.geni.co
2 CONC m/people/Tongzhi-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%90%8C%E6%B2%BB%E5%B8%9D/6000000008737146186 Emperor Mu-tsung] (Tsai-ch'un, q.v.). He was the second son of [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-Prince-Chun/6000
2 CONC 000009796546841 Prince I-huan] [q.v.]; his mother was a younger sister of [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] [q.v.]. At the age of two ''
2 CONC sui'' he was given the decorations of a first grade official. Early in 1874, at three ''sui'', he was allowed to wear the peacock feather. Late in 1874 he was given the salary of a prince of the sixth degree. On January 12, 1875, 
2 CONC [https://www.geni.com/people/Tongzhi-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%90%8C%E6%B2%BB%E5%B8%9D/6000000008737146186 Emperor Mu-tsung] died and [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/
2 CONC 6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] chose her nephew, [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien], as successor to the throne, adopting him a
2 CONC s her son. This selection violated the dynastic laws of succession (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in]) and was made solely in order to
2 CONC  maintain her power. Of all the princes, he was the nearest to her by blood; since he was still a child she could mould his career at will. Foreseeing the objection that this arrangement would leave the deceased [https://www.geni.
2 CONC com/people/Tongzhi-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%90%8C%E6%B2%BB%E5%B8%9D/6000000008737146186 Emperor Mu-tsung] without an heir, she promised that [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E
2 CONC 7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien]'s first son would be [https://www.geni.com/people/Tongzhi-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%90%8C%E6%B2%BB%E5%B8%9D/6000000008737146186 Mu-tsung]'s heir (see Tsai-Ch'un and Wu K'o-
2 CONC tu). Thus, at four ''sui'', [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] became the pawn of an ambitious, unscrupulous woman who undertook to contr
2 CONC ol his whole life, even to depriving him, in advance, of his rightful heir. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The decision making [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] heir to the throne was taken in the evening of January 12, 1875. Shortly after thr
2 CONC ee o'clock the next morning the child arrived at the Palace. On January 15 it was announced, in his name, that the Dowager Empresses, [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/600000000
2 CONC 9796777603 Hsiao-ch'in] and [https://www.geni.com/people/%E6%B8%85-%E5%AD%9D%E8%B2%9E%E9%A1%AF%E7%9A%87%E5%90%8E-%E6%85%88%E5%AE%89-%E9%88%95%E7%A5%9C%E7%A5%BF/6000000009796838324 Hsiao-chên] (see under the former), would rule as 
2 CONC co-regents during his minority. On February 25, 1875, he ascended the throne. The title '''Kuang-hsü''' 光緒 was used to designate the years of his reign which lasted nominally from January 6, 1875 to January 21, 1909. The two Dowag
2 CONC er Empresses ruled jointly as regents from 1875 to 1881 when [https://www.geni.com/people/%E6%B8%85-%E5%AD%9D%E8%B2%9E%E9%A1%AF%E7%9A%87%E5%90%8E-%E6%85%88%E5%AE%89-%E9%88%95%E7%A5%9C%E7%A5%BF/6000000009796838324 Hsiao-chên] died;
2 CONC  and then, from 1881 to 1887, [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in] ruled alone. On February 7, 1887 [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of
2 CONC -Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] was proclaimed to be of age, but the power to rule was withheld from him for two years more during which [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager
2 CONC -Cixi-慈禧太后/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in] "instructed him in state affairs" (''hsün-chêng'' 訓政). It was not until March 4, 1889 that she retired to the Summer Palace (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-慈
2 CONC 禧太后/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in]). Even so, she did not relinquish the reading of important state documents or the making of appointments to certain offices. [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85
2 CONC %E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] had no power to veto her decisions, for from childhood he had been taught to respect, to fear, and to obey her. He was married on February 26, 1889 to a daughter of a mat
2 CONC ernal uncle—to a cousin who was three years his senior and for whom he had no affection. Obviously, [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Hsiao-ch'in] a
2 CONC rranged this marriage, just prior to her retirement, in order that through her niece she could continue to exercise authority over the Emperor, and obtain confidential information on all proposed governmental policies. The hapless
2 CONC  bride, known as [https://www.geni.com/people/%E6%B8%85-%E5%AD%9D%E5%AE%9A%E6%99%AF%E7%9A%87%E5%90%8E-%E9%9A%86%E8%A3%95-%E8%91%89%E8%B5%AB%E9%82%A3%E6%8B%89-%E4%B8%80/6000000009796950539 Empress Hsiao-ting] (孝定景皇后, 1868-1913), la
2 CONC ter held the position of Dowager Empress (Lung-yü Huang-t'ai-hou 隆裕皇太后) from 1908 until her death. At the time of his marriage [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000
2 CONC 009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] was given as concubines two sisters: [https://www.geni.com/people/%E7%91%BE%E5%A6%83-%E4%BB%96%E5%A1%94%E5%96%87%E6%B0%8F/6000000011400492929 Chin-fei 瑾妃] (1874-1924) and [https://www.geni.com/people/%E7%8
2 CONC F%8D%E5%A6%83-%E4%BB%96%E5%A1%94%E5%96%87/6000000011400682466 Chên-fei 珍妃] (known to Westerners as the "Pearl Concubine", 1876-1900). They were cousins of Chih-jui [q.v.], and both had been tutored by Wên T'ing-shih [q.v.]. [https
2 CONC ://www.geni.com/people/%E7%8F%8D%E5%A6%83-%E4%BB%96%E5%A1%94%E5%96%87/6000000011400682466 Chên-fei], young and active, was perhaps the only woman [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%
2 CONC 92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] ever loved. She tried once, in 1894, to compete with Hsiao-ch'in in influencing appointments to official posts, but caused such a tempest in the Palace that she never tried again. Hsiao-
2 CONC ch'in ordered that she be degraded in rank, and though rank was later restored to her, Chên-fei was never forgiven for her temerity. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Under such unfavorable circumstances, [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien], surprisingly enough, grew up to be a man of considerable charac
2 CONC ter. For this, credit must be given to Wêng T'ung-ho [q.v.] , one of his tutors and his closest friend. It is said that as a child he was afraid of thunder and often, when he heard it, would hide his head in Wêng's lap. Wêng's dia
2 CONC ry is a valuable record of the Emperor's schooling. Besides the usual subjects, [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] studied English under 
2 CONC two former students of the T'ung-wên Kuan (see under Tung Hsün), namely, Shên To 沈鐸 and Chang Tê-i 張德彝 (T. 在初, 1847-1919), Minister to Great Britain, 1901-05. He was described as being an impetuous and ill-tempered child, but unde
2 CONC r Wêng's patient guidance he became a conscientious man with strong moral convictions. It is likely that he could have become an outstanding ruler but for his fear of his foster mother, Hsiao-ch'in—a fear which she had deliberatel
2 CONC y and firmly planted in his mind. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Hundred Days' Reform of 1898 (see under T'an Ssŭ-t'ung) [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] rebelled against her domination, bu
2 CONC t when Yüan Shih-k'ai (see under Yüan Chia-san) divulged the plot to overthrow her, she and the conservatives anticipated the coup. She returned to Peking from her retirement in the Summer Palace on September 22, 1898, and confine
2 CONC d [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] in a pavillion known as Ying-t'ai 瀛臺, on an island in the Imperial Park west of the Palace. In his n
2 CONC ame she issued decrees denouncing the reformers and begging herself to resume control of the government. Rumors of his illness were spread, and physicians were regularly summoned to lend support to the story. There was a plot eith
2 CONC er to dethrone him, or o put him to death, but because of covert protests from Liu K'un-i [q.v.] and other provincial officials, and veiled warnings from foreign diplomats, it was not carried out. However, on January 24, 1900, Hsi
2 CONC ao-ch'in and her protégé, Tsai-i (see under I-tsung), appointed the latter's son as Heir Apparent, not to [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'i
2 CONC en], but to the preceding Emperor, Mu-tsung. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Attributing the frustration of their plan to dethrone [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] to the influence of foreigners, Hsiao-ch'in and 
2 CONC Tsai-i decided to drive them out of China by force [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] knew the folly of making war on the Powers, but his
2 CONC  protests were ignored. The Empress proeeded to sponsor the anti-foreign mob known as Boxers, thus precipitating, in 1900, the Boxer Uprising (see under Jung-lu). Finally, early in the morning of August 14, foreign troops were rep
2 CONC orted to be entering the capital. Hsiao-ch'in decided to flee, but she would not leave [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] behind. His fav
2 CONC orite consort, Chên-fei, courageously suggested that the Emperor be left in Peking to negotiate a peace; but for her presumption she was by Hsiao-ch'in's order thrown down a well. Surrounded as he was by her supporters, [https://w
2 CONC ww.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] had no choice but to accompany the Empress Dowager. However, had he managed to remain in Peking, he could have g
2 CONC ained control of the throne, and the Empress might have been barred from resuming her power. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the flight and the stay in Sian (see under Hsiao-ch'in), [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] was granted more freedom than in the p
2 CONC receding two years. He was under surveillance, but was allowed some voice in matters of state. Hsiao-ch'in used him, however, as a scapegoat to issue decrees in which he supposedly blamed himself and the officials at Court for the
2 CONC  fiasco of the Boxer War. As soon as she was assured that her position would suffer no change, she ignored him. After their return to Peking she delegated to him only ceremonial duties and he enjoyed little more freedom than durin
2 CONC g his confinement. Nevertheless he patiently prepared himself for the day when his foster mother should die and leave him a free hand. He continued to read books on national and foreign affairs, and even resumed his study of the E
2 CONC nglish language. Yet he was never permitted, for even one day, to rule by himself. Hsiao-ch'in died on November 15, 1908; the death of [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D
2 CONC /6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] being announced as having occurred on the preceding day. He was given the posthumous title, '''Ching Huang-ti''' 景皇帝, and the temple name, '''Tê-tsung''' 德宗. His tomb, the last one erected in the C
2 CONC h'ing imperial burial grounds southwest of Peking, was named Ch'ung-ling 崇陵, where he was buried in 1913. Probably he did not die a natural death. Some writers conjecture that he was murdered after Hsiao-ch'in died, but that his d
2 CONC ecease was announced first, in order to disguised the sequence of events.  However this may be, before she died, Hsiao-ch'in named as successor to the childless [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%
2 CONC E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] his nephew, and her grand-nephew, P'u-i 溥儀 (b. Feb. 7, 1906), then three ''sui''. P'u-i was the son of [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85
2 CONC %E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien]'s younger brother, Tsai-fêng (see under I-huan), and his mother was a daughter of Jung-lu [q.v.] . He was treated as the adopted son of both [https://www.geni.com/people/
2 CONC Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] and Mu-tsung—a dual system of relationships known in China as ''chien-t'iao'' 兼祧. His own father, Tsai-fêng, was named by Hsiao-ch'
2 CONC in to serve as regent during his minority, to rule in co-operation with [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien]'s widow, the above-mentioned E
2 CONC mpress Hsiao-ting. P'u-i ruled for three years (January 22, 1909-February 17, 1912), under the reign title of Hsüan-t'ung 宣統. On February 12, 1912 his father and his foster mother agreed for him to abdicate the throne in favor of 
2 CONC a republican form of government, thus ending the Ch'ing Dynasty which had ruled China for 268 years. Though no longer Emperor, he was permitted to live in the Palace in Peking. In 1917 an ambitious general, Chang Hsün 張勳 (T. 少軒 H.
2 CONC  松壽老人, 1854-1923), staged an ill-fated coup d'état and restored P'u-i to the throne (July 1), but twelve days later (July 12) was defeated by republican forces and P'u-i again abdicated. In 1924 he was forced by General Fêng Yü-hs
2 CONC iang (see under Sung Ch'ing) to vacate the Palace. Thereafter he lived in the Japanese Concession in Tietsin until 1932 when he became the nominal head of the Japanese sponsored Manchurian regime. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There are many stories concerning the tragic life of [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien], some of which may well be true but cannot be sub
2 CONC stantiated by the facts at hand. Others are clearly fantastic. Statements to the effect that he was stupid or even feeble-minded were invented and circulated, probably by ambitious princes and officials who planned to dethrone him
2 CONC  in 1900. Der-ling (see under Hsiao-ch'in) who lived in the palace for two years as lady-in-waiting to the Empress Dowager, states that he was intelligent, studious, fond of music, and well-informed on a variety of subjects. His t
2 CONC ragedy was that he could not escape the control of his foster mother without breaking the conventional rules of filial piety. Although the actual power, throughout the thirty-three years of his reign, was in the hands of Hsiao-ch'
2 CONC in, the official history of those years is recorded under [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien]'s name and is entitled ''Tê-tsung Ching Huan
2 CONC g-ti shih-lu'' (實錄), 579 + 4 ''chüan''. It was completed in 1921 and is accompanied by a collection of edicts, entitled ''Tê-tsung Ching Huang-ti shêng-hsün'' (聖訓), 145 ''chüan''. He has credited to him also a collection of notes 
2 CONC on Chinese historical events, entitled 讀史隨筆 ''Tu-shih sui-pi'', 4 ''chüan''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [ 1/23-35; Wêng T'ung-ho, ''Wêng Wên-kung kung jih-chi'', vols. 13-40; Li Tz'u-ming [q.v.], ''Yüeh-man t'ang jih-chi'', vols. 21-36; ''Ch'ing Huang-shih ssu-p'u'' (see under Fu-lung-an); Der-ling, ''Two Years in the Forbidden City
2 CONC '' (1911); Johnston, R.F., ''Twilight in the Forbidden City'' (1934), chapters 1-10; Reid, John Gilbert, ''The Manchu Abdication and the Powers'', 1908-1912 (1935); Yün Yü-ting, ''Ch'ung-ling ch'uan-hsin lu'' ("Stories Relating to
2 CONC  [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien]") in 庸言 ''Yung-yen'', vol.2 (1914), and in 青鶴 ''Ch'ing-ho'', vol.5 (1936); idem, ''Tz'u-hsi chuan-hsi
2 CONC n lu chai-ch'ao'' ("Selected Stories Relating to Hsiao-ch'in"), in ''Ch'ing-ho'', vol.5 (1936-37); Wang Chao 王照, 德宗遺事 ''Tê-tsung i-shih''; Ch'ü Kuei-t'ing, ''Chên-chih Kuang-hsü Huang-ti mi-chi'' ("Diagnosis of the Illness of Kuan
2 CONC g-hsü, Privately Recorded") in 遺事 ''I-ching'', no. 29 (May 1937); Chao Ping-lin, ''Kuang-hsü ta-shih hui-chien'' (see bibl. under Yüan Chia-san).] 
2 CONT
2 CONT FANG CHAO-YING
