1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''I-huan''' 奕譞 (H. 樸庵), Oct. 16, 1840-1891, Jan. 1, the first '''Prince Ch'un''' (醇親王) was the seventh son of [https://www.geni.com/people/Daoguang-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E9%81%93%E5%85%89%E5%B8%9D/600000
2 CONC 0008737149905 Emperor Hsüan-tsung] (Min-ning, q.v.). He was the father of [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Emperor Tê-tsung] (Tsai-t'ien, q.v.) and
2 CONC  grandfather of the child emperor, P'u-i (Hsüan-t'ung, see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien]). His mother was a concubine who also
2 CONC  gave birth to [https://www.geni.com/people/Daoguang-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E9%81%93%E5%85%89%E5%B8%9D/6000000008737149905 Emperor Hsüan-tsung]'s eighth and ninth sons (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Daoguang-Empe
2 CONC ror-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E9%81%93%E5%85%89%E5%B8%9D/6000000008737149905 Min-ning]). In 1850, when his half-brother, [https://www.geni.com/people/Tongzhi-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%90%8C%E6%B2%BB%E5%B8%9D/600000000873714
2 CONC 6186 I-chu] [q.v.], ascended the throne, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] was created a prince of the second degree (Chün-wang 郡王) with the designation, Ch'un. In 1
2 CONC 859, at twenty ''sui'', he was given a mansion of his own—a palace which came to be known as Ch'i-yeh fu 七爺府, or "Palace of the Seventh Prince". It belonged originally to I-hui [q.v.], the poet. In 1861 after his nephew, [https://
2 CONC www.geni.com/people/Tongzhi-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%90%8C%E6%B2%BB%E5%B8%9D/6000000008737146186 Emperor Mu-tsung], ascended the throne, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/600000000979
2 CONC 6546841 I-huan] was made lieutenant-general of a Banner, an adjutant general, and a chamberlain of the Imperial Bodyguard. Early in 1862 he became assistant to his half-brother, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yixin-%E5%A5%95%E8%A8%A
2 CONC 2-1st-Prince-Gong/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] [q.v.], who commanded the Peking Field Force. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Since his wife was the younger sister of the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager (Hsiao-ch'in)] [q.v.], [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5
2 CONC %A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] was favored by that [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Empress] and was used by her to counteract 
2 CONC the power of [https://www.geni.com/people/Yixin-%E5%A5%95%E8%A8%A2-1st-Prince-Gong/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]. Thus in 1865, after [https://www.geni.com/people/Yixin-%E5%A5%95%E8%A8%A2-1st-Prince-Gong/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] 
2 CONC was deprived of his authority as Prince-Counselor, the supervision of the child emperor's education was entrusted to [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan]. In 1873, when
2 CONC  [https://www.geni.com/people/Tongzhi-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%90%8C%E6%B2%BB%E5%B8%9D/6000000008737146186 Emperor Mu-tsung] took over the government from his mother, he raised [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%
2 CONC 95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan]'s princedom to the first degree, and thus gave him a rank equal to that of [https://www.geni.com/people/Yixin-%E5%A5%95%E8%A8%A2-1st-Prince-Gong/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]. 
2 CONC After [https://www.geni.com/people/Tongzhi-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%90%8C%E6%B2%BB%E5%B8%9D/6000000008737146186 Emperor Mu-tsung] died early in 1875 leaving no heir, [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%
2 CONC 85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] announced at a meeting of the princes and high officials that [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9
2 CONC D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] (q.v., son of [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] and her own sister) would succeed to the throne. Though the choice was contrary to 
2 CONC the dynasty's unwritten rules of succession, the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Empress] resorted to this expedient to preserve her power, and no one dare
2 CONC d to interpose objections. [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] thus found himself in a difficult position, one in which he might easily be involved in intrigues. Accor
2 CONC ding to Wêng T'ung-ho [q.v.]—who was present at the meeting—when [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] heard the announcement he was so overcome that he wept and fainted
2 CONC . He pleaded serious illness and memorialized on his desire to be relieved of all service at Court. His request was granted, for by remaining at Court he would have to kneel to his own son—a point required by Court etiquette, but 
2 CONC repugnant to paternal instincts. He was therefore ordered to supervise the building of the tomb of [https://www.geni.com/people/Tongzhi-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%90%8C%E6%B2%BB%E5%B8%9D/6000000008737146186 Emperor Mu-tsun
2 CONC g], and his princedom was made "perpetually inheritable". 
2 CONT
2 CONT But [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan]'s retirement was only temporary, for in 1876 he was given supervision of the education of [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-
2 CONC Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Emperor Tê-tsung], then aged six (''sui''); and four years later was placed in full charge of the Peking Field Force. In 1884, when [https://www.geni.c
2 CONC om/people/Yixin-%E5%A5%95%E8%A8%A2-1st-Prince-Gong/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] was discharged from all offices, it was announced that [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-hua
2 CONC n] should be consulted on all important affairs of state. As China was then engaged in a conflict with France (see under Fêng Tzê-ts'ai), [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I
2 CONC -huan] took a prominent part in shaping the military program. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the first fleet of Chinese warships (see under Shên Pao-chên) was destroyed by the French in 1884, it was decided to build a new fleet. For that purpose the Board of Admiralty (Hai-chün Ya-mên 海軍衙門) was created by an order o
2 CONC f [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] issued on October 13, 1885. [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6
2 CONC 000000009796546841 I-huan] was appointed controller of this Board; I-k'uang (see under Yung-lin) and Li Hung-chang [q.v.] being made associate controllers, and Shan-ch'ing 善慶 (T. 厚齋, d. 1889, posthumous name 勤敏) and Tsêng Chi-tsê 
2 CONC [q.v.] assistant controllers. In May 1886 [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] and Li Hung-chang, escorted by warships, made a tour of the fortified areas at Taku, Port
2 CONC  Arthur, and Wei-hai-wei, inspecting coast defenses and observing naval maneuvers. [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] was much impressed by this show of strength, and
2 CONC  in the course of the tour wrote a number of poems which he published (1887) under the title 航海吟草 ''Hang-hai yin-ts'ao''. His enthusiasm for the building of a navy was genuine, and he stood behind Li Hung-chang in most of that sta
2 CONC tesman's plans for its improvement and expansion. He also helped other provincial officials in their efforts to introduce Western industries to China (see under Chang Chih-tung). But, like many of the Manchu princes at the close o
2 CONC f the dynasty, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] lacked decisiveness and could not stand against policies of the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E
2 CONC 6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] which he knew to be ill-advised. [https://www.geni.com/people/Yixin-%E5%A5%95%E8%A8%A2-1st-Prince-Gong/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] had been pliant enough befo
2 CONC re her strong will, but [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] was more so, consciously or unconsciously becoming her tool. He permitted her to use his son to further her
2 CONC  own ends, and looked on complacently when in 1886-91 she diverted funds, ear-marked for naval construction, to build for herself the Summer Palace known as the I-ho yüan (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cix
2 CONC i-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in]). During the naval maneuvers of 1886 he allowed her favorite eunuch, Li Lien-ying (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%
2 CONC 88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in]) to accompany him throughout the tour. After [https://www.geni.com/people/Yixin-%E5%A5%95%E8%A8%A2-1st-Prince-Gong/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] was stripped of 
2 CONC power [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in] freely sold offices to the highest bidders and shut her eyes to many other corrupt practices. Despite th
2 CONC ese delinquencies [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] repeatedly affirmed his loyalty to her. When in 1886 [https://www.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%
2 CONC E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Emperor Tê-tsung] came of age and had the right to rule independently, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] plea
2 CONC ded with her to continue her regency for three years more. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1887 [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] was seriously ill for several months. Every time his son (the Emperor) visited him the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress
2 CONC -Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] contrived to be present, and elaborate precautions had to be taken not to arouse her suspicions. In 1888 [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%
2 CONC E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] presented to the throne the residence where the emperor was born, perhaps to reassure her that he was content to live merely as a prince though his son had nominal cont
2 CONC rol of the government. In 1889, when the Emperor was married, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] was accorded various honors and his other sons were made princes of l
2 CONC ower degree. Soon after the marriage Wu Ta-ch'êng [q.v.] submitted a memorial to the Emperor, suggesting that the status of [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] as the 
2 CONC father of the emperor be clarified and that he be given the privileges pertaining to his position. The emperor referred the matter to the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/60000
2 CONC 00009796777603 Empress Dowager] who revealed a hitherto unpublished memorial of [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] (allegedly submitted by him in 1875) to the effect 
2 CONC that he wished his status to remain that of a servant to the throne and that he renounced all rights as father of the emperor. Some writers take the view that the memorial was written for the occasion and predated. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death, early in 1891, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] was canonized as '''Hsien''' 賢. His full posthumous title reads: 皇帝本生考醇賢親王 ''Huang-ti Pên-Shêng K'a
2 CONC o Ch'un Hsien Ch'in-wang'' or "The Emperor's Father-before-adoption, Prince Ch'un the Wise". In his home a temple was erected to celebrate his memory and there the sacrificial ceremonies were like those accorded to a deceased empe
2 CONC ror. But publicly and officially he was honored only as a prince. His tomb was of the kind ordinarily erected to a prince, and thus he was celebrated in the Imperial Ancestral Hall. After his grandson, P'u-i (see under [https://ww
2 CONC w.geni.com/people/Guangxu-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E5%85%89%E7%B7%92%E5%B8%9D/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien]), came to the throne [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I
2 CONC -huan] was referred to as ''Huang-ti pên-shêng tsu'' (祖) ''k'ao'', or "The Emperor's Grandfather-before-adoption". 
2 CONT
2 CONT An episode recorded in 1897 in the diary of Wêng T'ung-ho [q.v.], and presumably true, gives evidence of the unjust suspicion that lingered in the mind of the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5
2 CONC %A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager], even after [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] had died. Near his grave stood a giant gingko tree which, accordin
2 CONC g to fortune tellers, had the power to give the descendants of [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] permanent succession to the throne. When the [https://www.geni.com/p
2 CONC eople/Empress-Dowager-Cixi-%E6%85%88%E7%A6%A7%E5%A4%AA%E5%90%8E/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] heard of this she immediately ordered that the tree be cut down. Perhaps she felt that her fears were justified when snakes were 
2 CONC discovered in the trunk—snakes being associated with dragons in Chinese lore. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan]'s fifth son, Tsai-fêng 載灃, inherited the princedom in January 1891 and became the second Prince Ch'un. He went to Berlin in 1901 as
2 CONC  head of the mission that was sent to apologize for the murder of the German Minister in the Boxer Uprising of the previous year. He married a daughter of Jung-lu [q.v.] in 1902 and she gave birth to P'u-i in 1906. Early in 1908 T
2 CONC sai-fêng was made a Grand Councilor, and late in the same year—after his son, P'u-i, had been designated heir to the throne—he was named Prince Regent (攝政王) and later Regent (監國攝政王). Thus during the years 1908-13 he was the actual
2 CONC  ruler of China. He was, however, wholly incapable of meeting the changing political situation and the conflicting demands of the various factions—constitutionalists, revolutionaries, conservatives and Imperial Clansmen. In Octobe
2 CONC r 1911 a poorly organized band of soldiers carried out successfully an almost unpremeditated Revolution. The Ching dynasty came to an end, and Tsai-fêng agreed for his son to abdicate, and he himself retired to Tientsin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan]'s two younger sons—the sixth and seventh—belonged to a group of Imperial Clansmen who grasped important Cabinet posts and caused mu
2 CONC ch criticism from other factions. The sixth son, Tsai-hsün (see under I-chih) was a prince of the fifth degree when he was appointed in 1902 to take the place of Tsai-i (see under I-tsung) as the adopted son of I-chih [q.v.]. Thus
2 CONC  Tsai-hsün inherited the rank of ''beile'' or a prince of the third degree. In 1909 he was made head of the Commission for the Reorganization of the Navy and was sent to Europe to study naval affairs in various countries. In the s
2 CONC ummer of 1910 he visited Japan, and late in the same year was made Minister of the Navy. [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince-Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan]'s seventh son, Tsai-t'ao 載濤 (H. 野雲, b. 188
2 CONC 6?), who in 1902 took the place of Tsai-ying (see under I-hsin) as adopted son of I-ho (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Daoguang-Emperor-of-Qing-China-%E6%B8%85%E9%81%93%E5%85%89%E5%B8%9D/6000000008737149905 Min-ning]), was
2 CONC  to the army what his half-brother, Tsai-hsün, was to the navy. Late in 1908 the New Palace Guard was reorganized under the special command of Tsai-fêng with Tsai-t'ao as one of three superintendents. In 1909 Tsai-t'ao was made ch
2 CONC ief of the General Staff Council and in 1910 was sent to study military conditions in Japan, in the United States, and in European countries. Thus the three sons of [https://www.geni.com/people/Yihuan-%E5%A5%95%E8%AD%9E-1st-Prince
2 CONC -Chun/6000000009796546841 I-huan] virtually had full control of the government, but they were ignorant and tactless, inclined more to display their uniforms than to pursue intelligent action. During the Revolution of 1911 they han
2 CONC ded over their power to Yüan Shih-k'ai (see under Yüan Chia-san), and a few months later retired. In the early years of the Republic, Tsai-t'ao was given the title, General Kung-wei 鞏威將軍 and served on the Military Council of Gener
2 CONC als known as Chiang-chün-fu 將軍府. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [ 1/142/la; 1/177/26a; 1/227/lla; ''Ch'un Ch'in-wang I-huan chih Chün-chi-ch'u ch'ih-tu'' 致軍機處尺牘; 文獻叢編 ''Wên-hsien ts'ung-pien'' nos. 7-9; Tsêng Chi-tsê [q.v.], ''Kuei-p'u chai shih-ch'ao''; portraits of Tsai-hsün and Tsai-t'ao in
2 CONC  The Eastern Miscellany (''Tung fang tsa-chih'') vol. 7, no. 2 (February, 1910); McCormick, F., The Flowery Republic (1913); Heid, J. G., The Manchu Abdication and the Powers (1935); 中東戰紀本末 ''Chung Tung chuan-chi pên-mo'' (1R96) 1
2 CONC /23b; ''Wu K'o-chai nien-p'u'' (see under Wu Ta-ch'êng) pp. 176-86.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT FANG CHAO-YING 
