1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Jung-lu''' 榮祿 (T. 仲華), April 6, 1836-1903, April 11, official, was a member of the Manchu Plain White Banner. His clan name was Guwalgiya 瓜爾佳 and he was a descendant of Fiongdon [q.v.]. His grandfather, [https:/
2 CONC /www.geni.com/people/TSH/6000000009796874871 T'a-Ssu-ha 塔斯哈] (T. 秀泉, posthumous name 莊毅, d. 1830), served during the campaign of 1826-28 against the Mohammedans of Turkestan (see under Ch'ang-ling). In 1830, when T'a-Ssu-ha was as
2 CONC sistant military-governor at Kashgar, he was killed in action defending the frontiers against a Khokandian invasion. [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu]'s father, [https://www.geni.com/people/CS/6000000009
2 CONC 796668917 Ch'ang-shou 長壽] (T. 希彭, posthumous name 勤勇, d. 1852), a brigade-general, and [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu]'s uncle, [https://www.geni.com/people/CR/6000000013570943108 Ch'ang-jui 長瑞] (T. 小泉
2 CONC , posthumous name 武壯, d. 1852), also a brigade-general, were in joint command of a detachment that resisted the rebels under Hung Hsiu-ch'üan [q.v.] in Kwangsi, but both were killed in battle at Yung-an in that province, where a t
2 CONC emple was later erected to their memories. The biographies of the two brothers and of their father were compiled and printed by [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu], together with eulogies by his friends an
2 CONC d colleagues, some dated as late as 1890. This collection is entitled 世篤忠貞錄 ''Shih-tu chung-chên lu'', or 長白瓜爾佳氏三忠列傳 ''Ch'ang-pai Gua-êr-chia shih san-chung lieh-chuan''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1852, after his father had died in defense of the dynasty, [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] was made an honorary licentiate, and early in 1853 inherited the minor hereditary rank of Ch'i-tu-yü which 
2 CONC was posthumously given to his father. Later (1853) he was appointed a secretary in the Board of Works, and five years after that was made an assistant department director in the same Board. In 1859 he was transferred to the Board 
2 CONC of Revenue. When the British and French allied forces entered Peking in 1860 he served under [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] [q.v.], the renowned Prince Kung, and was in charge of police in the suburbs 
2 CONC of Peking. In 1861, in view of his contributions to the national treasury, he was given the rank of an expectant intendant of a circuit. Later in the same year he took part under [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885
2 CONC  I-hsin] and [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan] [q.v.] in the organization of the Peking Field Force (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]). This was the first army corps i
2 CONC n China equipped with modern firearms and drilled in the Western manner, It is said that in 1862 when the Dowager Empresses (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in]) returned fr
2 CONC om Jehôl it was [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] who escorted them with a loyal detachment of troops. In 1864 he became brigadier of one of the two wings of this force, which he led in 1865 to fight aga
2 CONC inst a band of outlaws from Manchuria, then operating in northeastern Chihli. In 1868 he was appointed lieutenant-general of the Gendarmerie patrolling the eastern part of Peking. Early in 1870 he was made general commandant of th
2 CONC e Peking Field Force, a post he held for nine years. Concurrently he served as vice-president of the Board of Works (1871-73), of the Board of Revenue (1873-78), president of the Board of Works (1878-79), a minister of the Imperia
2 CONC l Household (1873-79), and general commandant of the Peking Gendarmerie (1877-79). 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] was trusted by the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager, Hsiao-ch'in] [q.v.]. On the night of January 12, 1875, when she
2 CONC  decided to adopt her, nephew [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] [q.v.], as her son and to enthrone him as emperor, it was [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] whom she deputed 
2 CONC to lead an armed force to escort the child from the home of his father ([https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan]) to the Palace. At this time [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] was alre
2 CONC ady powerful, as commander of the Peking Gendarmerie and the Field Force. However, in September 1878 he was granted sick leave, and early in 1879 was retired from all his posts. According to [https://www.geni.com/people/WTH/600000
2 CONC 0018571653918 Wêng T'ung-ho] [q.v.], [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] had trouble with his leg which was operated on by a Western surgeon. According to another account, [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6
2 CONC 000000009796689882 Jung-lu] had incurred the displeasure of [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] by boasting of his influence over the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Do
2 CONC wager Empress, Hsiao ch'in]. Still another writer attributes his downfall to his having antagonized the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] by insisting on limiting the powers of 
2 CONC the eunuchs. Officially, however, he was accused of receiving bribes and was lowered two grades in rank. This meant that when he should be called to serve again he would receive a lower appointment than he had before his retiremen
2 CONC t. Although, in view of contributions he made toward the equipment of the army, he was restored to his former rank in 1885, he remained inactive for two years longer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1887 [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] was recalled from retirement and was appointed lieutenant-general of a Banner. A year later he was made a chamberlain of the Imperial Bodyguard From 1891 to 1894
2 CONC  he served as Tartar General of the Manchu garrison at Sian, Shênsi, where he organized a battalion of five hundred riflemen. In 1894 he was summoned to Peking to celebrate the sixtieth birthday of [https://www.geni.com/people/Emp
2 CONC ress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in]. But in view of the out-break of the Sino-Japanese War the celebration was not held. [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] was again made general co
2 CONC mmandant of the Peking Gendarmerie with instructions to maintain order in the capital. He was also detailed to serve in the Foreign Office known as the Tsungli Yamen. In 1895, after the close of the war with Japan, he was appointe
2 CONC d president of the Board of War and a year later was concurrently made an Associate Grand Secretary. During this period he devoted his energies to the training of a new army. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Sino-Japanese War there arose a strong demand for military reform. About the year 1895 Chang Chih-tung [q.v.] organized in-Nanking and Wuchang several battalions of troops equipped and trained in the Western way. At the 
2 CONC same time [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] recommended [https://www.geni.com/people/YSK/6000000011573123374 Yüan Shih-k'ai] (see under Yüan Chia-san) as capable of training a new army in Chihli. This ma
2 CONC rked the beginning of [https://www.geni.com/people/YSK/6000000011573123374 Yüan]'s military influence and laid the foundation of the so-called Pei-yang 北洋, or Northern, military party in China. Apart from [https://www.geni.com/peo
2 CONC ple/YSK/6000000011573123374 Yüan]'s new army, known as Hsin-Chien chün 新建軍, [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] sponsored the expansion of three other armies: the I-chün 毅軍 under Sung Ch'ing [q.v.]; the Ka
2 CONC nsu soldiers (甘軍) under Tung Fu-hsiang 董福祥 (T. 星五, 1839-1908); and the Wu-i chün 武毅軍 under Nieh Shih-ch'êng 聶士成 (T. 功亭, posthumous name 忠節, d. 1900). The forces of Sung and Tung consisted merely of old style soldiers without moder
2 CONC n firearms, whereas those under [https://www.geni.com/people/YSK/6000000011573123374 Yüan] and some of those under Nieh had modern training and equipment. The scramble of the Western powers for spheres of influence forced [https:/
2 CONC /www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] and other statesmen to become more military-minded, and caused [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor Tê-tsung] to launch the reform movement of June-Septem
2 CONC ber 1898 (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/TST/6000000069582008822 T'an Ssû-t'ung]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after the first reform decree was issued (June 11), the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Dowager Empress, Hsiao-ch'in], sensed the danger to herself of the [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/
2 CONC 6000000009796512974 Emperor]'s rising power and independence. To consolidate her position she needed the control of the military forces near Peking, and so on June 14 she effected the appointment of [https://www.geni.com/people/RL
2 CONC /6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] (then a full Grand Secretary) as governor-general of Chihli. While the reform movement was in progress she conspired with the reactionaries in Peking, and [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796
2 CONC 689882 Jung-lu] consolidated his control of the armies at Tientsin. Meanwhile the reformers also sought to gain control of the army, and on September 16 appointed [https://www.geni.com/people/YSK/6000000011573123374 Yüan Shih-k'ai
2 CONC ], an expectant vice-president of a Board, to take the place of [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] in command of the new forces. When [https://www.geni.com/people/YSK/6000000011573123374 Yüan] left Peking
2 CONC  he was empowered to execute [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] and to bring a force to Peking powerful enough to support the [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor] and relegate the 
2 CONC [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] to oblivion or to death. But when [https://www.geni.com/people/YSK/6000000011573123374 Yüan] reached Tientsin (September 20) he betrayed his tr
2 CONC ust and revealed the entire plan to [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu]. [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] left immediately for Peking and on the same day (September 20) had a co
2 CONC nference with the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Dowager Empress] and the reactionary courtiers. After a plan had been formulated he returned to Tientsin, brought his army to Peking, and on S
2 CONC eptember 22 supported the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] in her resumption of control of the government, in the confinement of [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/600000000979651
2 CONC 2974 Tê-tsung], and the rescinding of all edicts issued in the Hundred Days of Reform (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/TST/6000000069582008822 T'an Ssû-t'ung]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT For his faithful support of the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] was for a time given great powers in the governmen
2 CONC t similar to those granted the princes, [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] and [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan]. Late in September 1898 he was ordered to stay in Peking as a Gran
2 CONC d Councilor and as Grand Secretary, but was allowed to retain full control of all the military and naval forces of North China. Thus, at least in name, he became one of the most powerful ministers of the Ch'ing Dynasty. To increas
2 CONC e the efficiency of his command he reorganized the forces into an army corps designated Wu-wei chün 武衛軍. The corps was divided into five groups. The first army, called Wu-wei ch'ien chün (前軍) commanded by Nieh Shih-ch'êng was stat
2 CONC ioned at Lu-t'ai, northeast of Tientsin, for the defense of that city and the coast. The second, Wu-wei chün, under Tung Fu-hsiang, was stationed northeast of Peking The third, Wu-wei tso (左) chün, under Sung Ch'ing, was stationed
2 CONC  at Shanhaikuan. The fourth and most famous, Wu-wei yu (右) chün, under [https://www.geni.com/people/YSK/6000000011573123374 Yüan Shih-k'ai], was stationed at Hsiao-chan, southeast of Tientsin. The fifth army, Wu-wei chung (中) chün
2 CONC , created by [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu], and under his command, was stationed at Nan-yüan, the Imperial Hunting Park south of Peking. Thus [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-
2 CONC lu] was the founder of the new army of North China which, under the leadership of [https://www.geni.com/people/YSK/6000000011573123374 Yüan Shih-k'ai] and other officers, was the dominant military force in China until 1927. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At this time two movements, both aiming at the expulsion of foreigners and foreign influence, made their appearance in North China. One, known later as the Boxer Movement, arose among the common people; another arose among the nob
2 CONC les in Peking. The former had its origin in several mystic and superstitious organizations, notably the Ta-tao hui 大刀會, or Big Sword Society, and the Pa-kua chiao 八卦教 both claiming for their adherents supernatural powers, even to 
2 CONC immunity from bullets. During the Sino-Japanese War many people in Shantung joined these societies, believing that from them they could learn the magic which would save them from death by bullets. Led by shrewd and opportunistic r
2 CONC ascals, these destitute and ignorant farmers began to stage riots and commit robberies. Believing that Christian converts used mystic foreign influences to oppress non-Christians, the rioters murdered Christians and burned their c
2 CONC hurches. Li Ping-hêng 李秉衡 (T. 鑑堂, 1830-1900), governor of Shantung from 1894 to 1897 during the Sino-Japanese War and during the German occupation of Tsingtao, at first sent the perfect, [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/60000000097
2 CONC 66015749 Yü-hsien 毓賢] (T. 佐臣, d. 1901), to suppress the rioters by force (1895-96). But Li and [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009766015749 Yü-hsien] both hated foreigners for their political and military aggressions. In 18
2 CONC 95 Li memorialized the throne against the establishment of railroads, mines, telegraph lines, paper currency, factories, a modern army and navy, Westernized schools, and even the post office. The only foreign thing which he did no
2 CONC t condemn was firearms which could be used to oppose foreign aggression. In 1897 Li was succeeded by [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009766015749 Yü-hsien] who was even less enlightened snd may really have believed in the s
2 CONC upernatural powers claimed by the mobs. Both were responsible for sponsoring the secret societies which this time were known generally as I-ho ch'uan 義和拳, whence the name "Boxers". In 1899 the Boxers adopted the slogan, "Support t
2 CONC he Ch'ings, Annihilate Foreigners" (扶清滅洋), thus courting the approval of such anti-foreign reactionaries as [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009766015749 Yü-hsien] who gave his official endorsement to the movement by changin
2 CONC g its name to I-ho t'uan (團). After many churches had been burnt and missionaries murdered, the foreign ministers in Peking succeeded, late in 1890, in having [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009766015749 Yü-hsien] removed f
2 CONC rom Shantung  and [https://www.geni.com/people/YSK/6000000011573123374 Yüan Shih-k'ai] sent in his place. [https://www.geni.com/people/YSK/6000000011573123374 Yüan] took his army to Shantung and suppressed the Boxers by force. For
2 CONC bidden in Shantung, the Boxers gradually moved to Chihli where they were welcomed and sponsored by the governor-general, [https://www.geni.com/people/%E7%A5%BF-%E8%A3%95/6000000011589634647 Yü-lu 裕祿] (T. 壽山, d. 1900). By May 1900 
2 CONC one group of Boxers had established itself in Tientsin and another advanced northward to combine with the anti-foreign group in Peking. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The anti-foreign movement among the uneducated nobles and superstitious courtiers may be said to have been led by the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] who was irked by the supp
2 CONC ort which the foreign governments had rendered to the reformers of 1898. [https://www.geni.com/people/ZY/6000000009824253093 Prince Tuan] (Tsai-i, see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009824088033 I-tsung]) despised th
2 CONC e foreigners because they had frustrated his plan to elevate his own son to the throne in place of [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor Tê-tsung]. He saw in the Boxers an instrument for getting rid of foreig
2 CONC ners, and convinced the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] that the Boxers had unusual powers. Other princes who favored the Boxers were [https://www.geni.com/people/ZX/600000000
2 CONC 9823465850 Tsai-hsün] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YL/6000000009824054343 Yin-lu]) and [https://www.geni.com/people/ZY/6000000009824253093 Tsai-i]'s younger brother, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZL/6000000009824423478 T
2 CONC sai-lan] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009824088033 I-tsung]), who were in turn encouraged by other reactionaries like Li Ping-hêng; the Grand Secretary, [https://www.geni.com/people/XT/6000000011495915939 Hsü 
2 CONC T'ung 徐桐] (T. 豫如 H. 蔭軒, 仲琴, 1819-1900); the Associate Grand Secretary, Kang-i 剛毅 (T. 子良, d. 1900); the President of the Board of Punishments, Chao Shu-ch'iao 趙舒翹 (T. 展如, d. 1900); the President of the Board of Ceremonies, Ch'i-hsi
2 CONC u 啟秀 (T. 穎芝 d. 1900); and the Vice-president of the Board of Revenue, Ying-nien 英年 (T. 菊儕, d. 1901). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In this period of conflicting counsels [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] did not assert himself. He knew that the Boxers could not be trusted, and that armed conflict with the Powers was futile. Yet he d
2 CONC id not dare to oppose the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager], and feared to offend [https://www.geni.com/people/ZY/6000000009824253093 Tsai-i]. Moreover, he had personal grudges 
2 CONC against [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor Tê-tsung]. Among his subordinates, [https://www.geni.com/people/YSK/6000000011573123374 Yüan Shih-k'ai] and Nieh Shih-ch'êng recognized the Boxers as a lawless mo
2 CONC b. But Tung Fu-hsiang and his unruly Mohammedan soldiers from Kansu who were stationed in or near Peking were openly anti-foreign, and on June 11 murdered a secretary of the Japanese Legation. This was two days before the Boxers w
2 CONC ere invited into Peking by order of [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] and [https://www.geni.com/people/ZY/6000000009824253093 Tsai-i], and three days before the Boxers enter
2 CONC ed Tientsin. Later Tung's soldiers in Peking joined the Boxers in pillaging, burning and murdering. Though nominally in command, [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] was powerless and had to accede to the e
2 CONC dicts which justified the course taken by the Boxers. On June 20, at a council of princes and officials—from which [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] was absent—[https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowage
2 CONC r-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] and [https://www.geni.com/people/ZY/6000000009824253093 Tsai-i], overruling the opposition from more enlightened officials, declared war on the foreign powers and initiated the attac
2 CONC k on the foreign Legations in Peking. As commander of the army, [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] had to issue the orders to the soldiers under Tung Fu-hsiang who made the attack. He probably foresaw the
2 CONC  folly of these actions, but was in danger of losing his own life if he voiced strong opposition. He kept silent, obeyed the commands of the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager], b
2 CONC ut secretly prepared for the eventuality of victory by the foreigners. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 9 Nieh Shih-ch'êng, who fought against the Boxers and was also attacked by the Allied Forces, was killed while defending Tientsin. Five days later the Allied Forces took that city. The Court in Peking became alarmed. Most 
2 CONC of the time from July 15 to August 6 attacks on the Legations were suspended. The foreign ministers were invited to go to Tientsin under the escort of [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu], but naturally dec
2 CONC lined to leave their barricades. In the meantime the out-spoken anti-foreign conservative, Li Ping-hêng, reached Peking (about July 25) and by agreeing to fight against the foreigners greatly bolstered the spirit of [https://www.g
2 CONC eni.com/people/ZY/6000000009824253093 Tsai-i] and the other conservatives who on July 28 ordered the execution of two high officials, [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000051858086887 Yüan Ch'ang] and [https://www.geni.com/peopl
2 CONC e/XZC/6000000069446074954 Hsü Ching-ch'êng] [qq.v.] , for opposing [https://www.geni.com/people/ZY/6000000009824253093 Tsai-i]'s policies. The assault on the Legations was presently resumed. As the allied forces advanced northward
2 CONC  from Tientsin (August 5), they first overcame and dispersed the army under Sung Ch'ing. Governor-general [https://www.geni.com/people/%E7%A5%BF-%E8%A3%95/6000000011589634647 Yü-lu], who fled with the defeated army, committed suic
2 CONC ide. On August 11 Li Ping-hêng was defeated near Tungchow and he too committed suicide a day later. On August 14 the allied forces entered Peking, thus lifting the siege of the Legations. Nevertheless, so blood-thirsty were the mi
2 CONC sguided conservatives that only three days before Peking fell they ordered the execution of three other high officials for urging the suppression of the Boxers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] fled from Peking to set up Court at Sian she ordered [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu], Ch'ung-ch'i [q.v.]
2 CONC , and [https://www.geni.com/people/XT/6000000011495915939 Hsü T'ung] to remain in the capital to negotiate with the foreigners. But [https://www.geni.com/people/XT/6000000011495915939 Hsü] hanged himself and [https://www.geni.com/
2 CONC people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] and Ch'ung-ch'i fled to Paoting with a handful of Tung Fu-hsiang's Kansu soldiers. On August 26 Ch'ung-ch'i also committed suicide. [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu
2 CONC ] was ordered to command the troops at Paoting, and later it was decreed that he should assist Prince Ch'ing (I-k'uang, see under Yung-lin) and Li Hung-chang [q.v.] in the negotiations with the foreign envoys. But [https://www.gen
2 CONC i.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] did not dare to return to Peking. In October Li Hung-chang warned him, and the Court at Sian, that the foreign envoys regarded him as having been in command of the soldiers who attacked
2 CONC  the Legations. So he hurriedly left Paoting for Sian to serve in his capacity as head of the Grand Council. The negotiations were carried on by Li Hung-chang and I-k'uang. In the meantime the sponsors of the Boxers were punished;
2 CONC  [https://www.geni.com/people/%E7%AB%AF%E9%83%A1%E7%8E%8B-%E8%BC%89%E6%BC%AA-%E4%BA%8C/6000000009824253093 Tsai-i] and his brother, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZL/6000000009824423478 Tsai-lan] (see under [https://www.geni.com/peo
2 CONC ple/YC/6000000009824088033 I-tsung]), were banished to Ili; Ying-nien, Chao Shu-ch'iao and Tsai-hsun, were ordered to commit suicide. [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009766015749 Yü-hsien], who as governor of Shansi in 1900
2 CONC  ruthlessly killed many missionaries and Christian converts, was executed. So were Ch'i-hsiu and [https://www.geni.com/people/XT/6000000011495915939 Hsü T'ung]'s son, [https://www.geni.com/people/XCY/6000000011496145515 Hsü Ch'êng
2 CONC -yü 徐承煜] (T. 楠士, d. 1901). Even those who had already died—men like [https://www.geni.com/people/XT/6000000011495915939 Hsü T'ung], Kang-i, and Li Ping-hêng—were posthumously dishonored. Tung Fu-hsiang was deprived of all ranks an
2 CONC d offices. [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] was not condemned, but was commended for restraining Tung Fu-hsiang's soldiers during their attack on the Legations. Thus when the Court returned to Peking, e
2 CONC arly in 1902, he was made senior Grand Secretary in place of Li Hung-chang who had died two months previously. Afflicted, however, by a disease, [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] was inactive and died in
2 CONC  1903. He was canonized as Wên-chung 文忠 and was given posthumously the hereditary rank of a first class baron. His name was celebrated in the Temple of Eminent Statesmen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Opinions differ as to [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu]'s conduct during the Boxer War. In view of his strategic position with the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Em
2 CONC press Dowager] and his great powers and responsibility as presiding member of the Grand Council and as commander of the new army, he cannot be absolved for failing to stamp out the Boxers before they became so strong. He could hav
2 CONC e done it, as [https://www.geni.com/people/YSK/6000000011573123374 Yüan Shih-k'ai] did in Shantung, if he had issued the order before the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] endor
2 CONC sed the Boxers. This was possibly the only way by which the nemesis of 1900 could have been averted, though he might have lost his position in the process. Later he was praised by many writers for his attempts to stop the attack o
2 CONC n the Legations, and for refusing to the Boxers the use of certain artillery. Others maintain that he pleaded for the lives of [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000051858086887 Yüan Ch'ang] and other victims of the anti-foreign 
2 CONC clique, but he did not press his point after the [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] threatened him for intervention. What he actually did in these instances is not known. Some st
2 CONC atements in praise of his conduct are possibly based on a diary attributed to Ching-shan 景善 (1823-1900), a retired official who was murdered by his son shortly after the Allies entered Peking. The diary was found by an Englishman,
2 CONC  presumably E. Backhouse, on August 18, 1900, in the courtyard of Ching-shan when the residence of that official was about to be burned by Sikhs. The document purports to relate the events of the fateful days from May to August 19
2 CONC 00. It was translated into English and published in 1910 in Bland and Backhouse, ''China Under the Empress Dowager''. In 1924 a new translation, made by [https://www.geni.com/people/%E6%96%87%E9%81%94-%E6%88%B4/6000000056392353821
2 CONC  J. J. L. Duyvendak], was published, together with the Chinese text taken from the original document preserved in the British Museum. Recently, however, the diary has been shown, by [https://www.geni.com/people/%E6%96%87%E9%81%94-
2 CONC %E6%88%B4/6000000056392353821 Dr. Duyvendak] and others, to be a forgery compiled from various sources by one or more persons. According to Chin-liang (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/WTH/6000000018571653918 Wêng T'ung-ho])
2 CONC , who took an active part in editing the official history of the Ch'ing Dynasty, ''Ch'ing-shih-kao'' (characters in Yu T'ung), the motive of those who fabricated the document was to make [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/60000000097
2 CONC 96689882 Jung-lu] appear as a friend of foreigners and so clear him of any responsibility in connection with the attack on the Legations. In his miscellany, entitled 四朝佚聞 ''Ssu-ch'ao i-wên'' (1936), Chin-liang states that he had i
2 CONC ntended to include in the Official History a biography of Ching-shan, because of the latter's wide fame as the writer of the diary, but that a closer examination of the diary disclosed so many errors and discrepancies that he conc
2 CONC luded to omit the sketch. A comparison of the diary with known memorials shows that many statements in it which criticize the Boxers and favor foreigners were culled from those memorials and put into the mouth of [https://www.geni
2 CONC .com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu]. In Chin-liang's opinion, friends or adherents of [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu], anticipating that the wrath of the foreign powers would fall upon him, for
2 CONC ged the diary in order to clear him-and then placed it where observant foreigners would find it. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Chin-liang draws attention to a letter written by Tung Fu-hsiang to [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] in which Tung complains that though [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] orde
2 CONC red him to attack the foreigners, when punishment was finally demanded, he shifted the responsibility to Tung alone. Wang Yen-wei (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]) who was in Peking in 1900 an
2 CONC d who, as a secretary of the Grand Council, followed the Court to Sian, characterized [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] as talented, but dangerous, treacherous, and covetous. These characterizations are 
2 CONC hard to reconcile with the amiable figure portrayed in Ching-shan's diary, but they fit the character of one who may have ordered the compilation of such a diary. It cannot truthfully be said that [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6
2 CONC 000000009796689882 Jung-lu] was a great statesman, or that he ever pretended to be. He was more concerned with personal wealth and position than with national affairs. He owed his rise to power, not primarily to great personal mer
2 CONC its, but to the favors he received from [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] and from [https://www.geni.com/people/Empress-Dowager-Cixi/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in]. The former regarded him as a 
2 CONC protègé and the latter as a favorite and an obedient tool. Though during the years 1898-1903 he might have exercised a salutary influence in the government, he failed to use his power to further either the interests of the nation 
2 CONC or of the ruling house. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] was the father of the wife of the second [https://www.geni.com/people/ZF/6000000009796382188 Prince Ch'un], whose name was Tsai-fêng (see under [https://www.geni.com/peo
2 CONC ple/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan]), and he was the maternal grandfather of [https://www.geni.com/people/PY/6000000009796535956 P'u-i] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT [ 1/443/la; 2/57/33b; 6/1/19b (mistaken in date of [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu]'s death); ''Shih-tu chung-chên lu''; Fan Tsêng-hsiang 樊增祥, 樊山集 ''Fan-shan chi'' 23/92a; Morse, H. B., ''The Internatio
2 CONC nal Relations of the Chinese Empire'' (1918), vols. II, III; ''Chin-shih jên-wu chih'' (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/WTH/6000000018571653918 Wêng T'ung-ho]) p. 207; [https://www.geni.com/people/WGZ/6000000070405911844 Wê
2 CONC n Kung-chih 文公直], 最近三十年中國軍事史 ''Tsui-chin san-shih-nien Chung-kuo chün-shih shih'' (1930); 逸經 ''I-ching'', no. 22, pp. 25-28; 人文 ''Jên-wên'', vol. II, nos. 5, 10, vol. III, nos. 5, 7; ''Tung-hua lu'', ''Kuang-hsü''; Wang Yen-wei, '
2 CONC 'Hsi-hsün ta-shih chi'' (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]); ''Chung-kuo chin pai-nien shih tzu-liao'' (see under Li Hsiu-ch'êng) (first series, 1926; second series, 1933); 西巡回鑾始末記 ''Hsi-hsün hu
2 CONC i-luan shih-mo chi'' (1905); [https://www.geni.com/people/%E6%96%87%E9%81%94-%E6%88%B4/6000000056392353821 Duyvendak], "Ching-shan's Diary—a Mystification", in ''T'oung Pao'', 1937, pp. 268-94; Lewisohn, William, ''Some Critical N
2 CONC otes on the So-called "Diary of His Excellency Ching Shan"'', in ''Monumenta Serica'', vol. II (1936-37), pp. 191-202; Li Ping-hêng, 李忠節公奏議 ''Li Chung-chieh kung tsou-i'' (1930) 7/28b, 8/16b, 9/20b, 10/3b, 12/15b; ''U.S. Foreign R
2 CONC elations'' (1901), Appendix: Rockhill's Report on China; [https://www.geni.com/people/WTH/6000000018571653918 Wêng T'ung-ho] [q.v.], ''Wêng Wên-kung kung jih-chi'' (1925); Cordier, Henri, ''Histoire des relations de la Chine'', vo
2 CONC l. 3 (1902).] 
2 CONT
2 CONT FANG CHAO-YING
