2 _MARNM 永
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1752
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1823
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Yung-hsing''' 永瑆 (T. 鏡泉 H. 少厂, 即齋), Mar. 22, 1752-1823, May 7, the first '''Prince Ch'êng''' was the eleventh son of [https://www.geni.com/people/QL/6000000009796722780 Emperor Kao-tsung] and a brother of [https
2 CONC ://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009826162271 Yung-hsüan] [q.v.] by the same mother. From youth on he excelled in calligraphy, and for his skill in this field he evoked the [https://www.geni.com/people/QL/6000000009796722780 Emper
2 CONC or]'s admiration. In 1779 he was appointed a director-general of the ''Ssŭ-k'u'' Commission (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/JY/6000000019201828133 Chi Yün]). He accompanied his father on several tours, and in 1789 was made
2 CONC  a prince of the first degree with the designation, Ch'êng 成親王. In 1795 he served in the capacity of lieutenant-general of a Manchu Banner. Four years later, after the death of [https://www.geni.com/people/QL/6000000009796722780 E
2 CONC mperor Kao-tsung] he was named, by the succeeding [https://www.geni.com/people/JQ/6000000009796546943 Emperor Jên-tsung], a Grand Councilor and concurrently supervisor of the Board of Revenue. At the same time he was placed in cha
2 CONC rge of the Board of Civil Office. These three posts had, up to this time, been filled by the powerful minister, [https://www.geni.com/people/HS/6000000009797419358 Ho-shên] [q.v.], but after the latter was imprisoned [https://www.
2 CONC geni.com/people/JQ/6000000009796546943 Emperor Jên-tsung] ordered [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Yung-hsing] and [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009826162271 Yung-hsüan] to reorganize the administration
2 CONC  in such a way that the followers of [https://www.geni.com/people/HS/6000000009797419358 Ho-shên] could not again assume control. When the property of [https://www.geni.com/people/HS/6000000009797419358 Ho-shên] was confiscated, p
2 CONC art of his garden near the summer palace, Yüan-ming Yüan (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/QL/6000000009796722780 Hung-li]), was given to [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Yung-hsing]—another portion remain
2 CONC ed still in the hands of the deposed minister's son. The garden is now part of the site of the Yenching University campus. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After serving six months on the Board of Revenue, [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Yung-hsing] was released from his duties; and after another three months, was discharged from the Grand Council. His dismissal w
2 CONC as not due to incompetency, but to a practice of the dynasty not to entrust a prince with undue authority. It seems that [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Yung-hsing] was perhaps unintentionally involved in the c
2 CONC ase of [https://www.geni.com/people/HLJ/6000000049897548973 Hung Liang-chi] [q.v.] who had addressed to him a letter criticizing the government. Though [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Yung-hsing] at once passed
2 CONC  the letter on to the [https://www.geni.com/people/JQ/6000000009796546943 Emperor]—and thus effected the banishment of [https://www.geni.com/people/HLJ/6000000049897548973 Hung]—he could not himself escape a measure of suspicion. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After relinquishing all his important posts in the government [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Yung-hsing] devoted himself once more to calligraphy and poetry. He and his brother, Yung-jung (see under [https://w
2 CONC ww.geni.com/people/QL/6000000009796722780 Hung-li]), were also known as great painters in their day. In 1814 he was ordered by [https://www.geni.com/people/JQ/6000000009796546943 Emperor Jên-tsung] to select the best specimens of 
2 CONC his handwriting, to be inscribed on stone and reproduced in the form of rubbings. The [https://www.geni.com/people/JQ/6000000009796546943 Emperor] gave the collection of rubbings the title, 詒晉齋法帖 ''I-chin chai fa-t'ieh'', after th
2 CONC e name of the studio where [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Yung-hsing] stored a large collection of books and objects of art. In 1819, owing to an error he made in offering sacrifices at the Temple of Earth, [h
2 CONC ttps://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Yung-hsing] was deprived of all his posts and was made to pay a fine. He died four years later. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The literary works of [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Yung-hsing] bear the title, ''I-chin chai chi'' (集), 8 + 1 ''chüan''. There is also a supplement of miscellaneous notes entitled ''I-chin chai sui-pi'' (隨筆)
2 CONC  in 1 ''chüan''. This collection was originally printed during his lifetime and was twice reprinted. One reprinting was made in 1846 by his great-grandson, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZR/6000000009825899713 Tsai-jui 載銳] (d. 1859,
2 CONC  posthumous name 恭), who succeeded in 1823 to the reduced rank of a prince of the second degree (郡王) and became the second Prince Ch'êng. [https://www.geni.com/people/ZR/6000000009825899713 Tsai-jui]'s father and grandfather died 
2 CONC earlier than [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Yung-hsing]. Among [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Yung-hsing]'s grandsons the most illustrious was [https://www.geni.com/people/YJ/6000000012990
2 CONC 727029 I-ching] [q.v.]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The residence of [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Yung-hsing] in Peking, which was situated on the bank of the pond known as Shih-ch'a hai 十剎海, was originally the palace of [https://www.geni.com/people/MJ/600000
2 CONC 0009821674495 Mingju] [q.v.]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [ 71/13b; 1/227/4a; 19 ''ting hsia'' 8a; 29 ''shou'' 2b; ''Ch'ing Huang-shih ssŭ-p'u'' (see under Fu-lung-an); Hung, William, ''Ho-shên and Shu-ch'un-yüan; Ching-shih fang-hsiang chih'' (see bibl. under Ulgungga), 6/7a; L.T.C.L.H.
2 CONC M.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT FANG CHAO-YING 
