1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''A-k'o-tun''' 阿克敦 (T. 立軒, 沖和 H. 恆巖), May 4, 1685-1756, Feb. 22, official, was a member of the Chang-chia 章佳 clan and of the Manchu Plain Blue Banner. He was the father of [https://www.geni.com/people/Agui-Janggiy
2 CONC a/6000000010764096053 A-kuei] [q.v.]. After receiving the ''chü-jên'' degree in 1708 and his ''chin-shih'' in 1709, [https://www.geni.com/people/Akdun-Janggiya/6000000010764290467 A-k'o-tun] became successively a bachelor (1709-12
2 CONC ), a compiler (1712-15), an expositor (1715-16), and a reader (1716-17) in the Hanlin Academy. In 1717 he was sent as head of a mission to Korea, being re-appointed in 1722 and 1724. On all of these occasions he was well received,
2 CONC  owing in part to his impressive appearance and his dignified bearing. From 1718 to 1726 he served, among other posts, as sub-chancellor of the Grand Secretariat (1718-22), junior vice-president of the Board of War (1722-23), chan
2 CONC cellor of the Hanlin Academy (1722-25), and senior vice-president of the Board of Ceremonies (1726) and the Board of War (1726). When [https://www.geni.com/people/KYX/6000000012400041989 K'ung Yü-hsün 孔毓珣] (T. 東美, d. 1730, age 65 
2 CONC ''sui''), was summoned for an audience with the emperor in 1726 [https://www.geni.com/people/Akdun-Janggiya/6000000010764290467 A-k'o-tun] was sent to take his place as acting governor-general of Kwangtung and Kwangsi, and in addi
2 CONC tion was made Tartar General of Canton. In the following year he was transferred to the post of acting governor of Kwangtung, and later in the same year to that of Kwangsi. Unfortunately he did not get on well with his colleagues,
2 CONC  and in 1728 as the result of charges brought against him by [https://www.geni.com/people/KYX/6000000012400041989 K'ung Yü-hsün] and Yang Wên-ch'ien 楊文乾 (T. 元統 H. 霖宰, d. 1728, age 47 ''sui''), he was deprived of his office and tit
2 CONC les.
2 CONT
2 CONT Three years later (1731) he was reinstated as an extra sub-chancellor of the Grand Secretariat, serving in the army of the Northwest in a campaign against the Eleuths. The operations proved embarrassing to the government, however,
2 CONC  and in 1734 [https://www.geni.com/people/Akdun-Janggiya/6000000010764290467 A-k'o-tun] was made assistant to [https://www.geni.com/people/FN/6000000076983978563 Fu-nai 傅鼐] (T. 閣峰, member of the Fuca 富察 clan, d. ca. 1738, age 62 '
2 CONC 'sui''), in the peace negotiations held at the tribal headquarters at Ili. Failing to accomplish its aims, the Commission returned to Peking in the spring of 1735. But three years later (1738) [https://www.geni.com/people/Akdun-Ja
2 CONC nggiya/6000000010764290467 A-k'o-tun], in charge of another Commission, succeeded in concluding a boundary agreement. After his return to Peking, early in 1739, he filled many posts, among them the following: junior and senior vic
2 CONC e-president both of the Board of Works (1738-40) and of the Board of Punishments (1740); senior vice-president of the Board of Civil Office (1740-46), serving concurrently as lieutenant-general of the Chinese Plain White Banner (1
2 CONC 742) and of the Manchu Bordered Blue Banner (1743); chancellor of the Hanlin Academy (1745-48); and president of the Censorate (1746). He was also president of the Board of Punishments (1746-48), and Associate Grand Secretary (174
2 CONC 8). In 1748 an error was made in the Manchu translation of an edict conferring a posthumous title upon Empress Hsiao-hsien (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Mishan-Fuca/6000000010103943941 Mishan]). Since the edict was frame
2 CONC d by the Hanlin Academy of which [https://www.geni.com/people/Akdun-Janggiya/6000000010764290467 A-k'o-tun] was in charge, [https://www.geni.com/people/Akdun-Janggiya/6000000010764290467 A-k'o-tun] was deprived of his post, but re
2 CONC mained to serve as sub-chancellor of the Grand Secretariat. Soon after, he was re-appointed president of the Board of Punishments, serving concurrently as lieutenant-general of the Chinese Bordered White Banner, and later as chanc
2 CONC ellor of the Hanlin Academy. Early in 1749 he was reinstated as Associate Grand Secretary. Thereafter he served the government without interruption until his retirement in 1755 owing to trouble with his eyes. During his last years
2 CONC  of service, when the emperor was journeying to Jehôl, Honan, and Fêngtien, he was three times (1749, 1750, and 1754) entrusted with the conduct of affairs at the capital. He died early in 1756 and was canonized as '''Wên-ch'in'''
2 CONC  文勤.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Akdun-Janggiya/6000000010764290467 A-k'o-tun] was forthright in character, aligning himself with no political party. Though he and [https://www.geni.com/people/NGY/6000000010103693526 Nien Kêng-yao] [q
2 CONC .v.] were friends and fellow members of the Hanlin Academy, he declined to join [https://www.geni.com/people/NGY/6000000010103693526 Nien] when the latter was in power. He achieved high literary distinction and on many occasions w
2 CONC as in charge of examinations. He served as vice-director for the compilation of the second edition of the ''Ta-Ch'ing hui-tien'' (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/WAG/6000000068904705032 Wang An-kuo]), commissioned in 1724 a
2 CONC nd completed in 1733; of the first edition of the ''Pa-ch'i t'ung-chih'' (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/LF/6000000069832620871 Li Fu]); and of the first edition of the ''Ta Ch'ing i-t'ung-chih'' (see under [https://www.ge
2 CONC ni.com/people/XQX/6000000069836370427 Hsü Ch'ien-hsüeh]). His collected works, entitled 德蔭堂集 ''Tê-yin-t'ang chi'', 16 ''chüan'', were published in 1778 by his son, [https://www.geni.com/people/Agui-Janggiya/6000000010764096053 A-k
2 CONC uei], and later (1816) were reprinted, together with his ''nien-p'u'', 1 ''chüan'', compiled by his great-grandson, [https://www.geni.com/people/%E5%BD%A5%E6%88%90-Janggiya/6000000010764376232 Na-yen-ch'êng] [q.v.].
2 CONT
2 CONT [ 1/309/4b; 2/16/3b; 3/17/1a; 4/26/10a; 9/20/3a; 1/35/53b.]
2 CONT
2 CONT S. K. CHANG 
2 CONT
2 CONT J. C. YANG 
2 CONT --------------------
