1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://www.geni.com/people/YL/6000000009797921214 Yung-lin 永璘], June 17, 1766-1820, Apr. 25, the first '''Prince Ch'ing''' (慶親王), was the seventeenth son of Emperor Kao-tsung (see under Hung-li). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Mien-t'i died without a male heir, and a son of Mien-hsing named [https://www.geni.com/people/YK/6000000009796754104 '''I-k'uang''' 奕劻] (1836-1916), was in 1850 made heir to the family estate with the low title of a noble of the t
2 CONC enth rank. Gradually, however, [https://www.geni.com/people/YK/6000000009796754104 I-k'uang] rose to prominence. In 1884, after I-hsin was dismissed for maintaining too pacific a policy toward France, [https://www.geni.com/people/
2 CONC YK/6000000009796754104 I-k'uang] succeeded him as chief member of the Office of Foreign Affairs (Tsungli Yamen), and remained in that position for twenty-seven years until he became premier (1911). In 1884 [https://www.geni.com/pe
2 CONC ople/YK/6000000009796754104 I-k'uang] was made a prince of the second degree to which was attached the family designation, Ch'ing. In this way he became the fourth Prince Ch'ing. In 1885 he was appointed one of the controllers of 
2 CONC the Board of Admiralty—the other being I-huan [q.v.]. In 1894, when the Empress Dowager celebrated her sixtieth birthday, [https://www.geni.com/people/YK/6000000009796754104 I-k'uang] was raised to a prince of the first degree. Hi
2 CONC s power, however, was negligible and he did not dare to oppose the Empress Dowager and her ignorant advisers when they foolishly looked for help to the Boxers. When the Court fled from Peking in 1900 he also fled to Hsüan-hua, but
2 CONC  on August 26 was ordered to return to Peking to co-operate with Li Hung-chang [q.v.] in peace negotiations with the Allies. After he and Li signed the Protocol in 1901, ending the hostilities of the Boxer War, he continued to con
2 CONC duct foreign affairs. In 1903, after Jung-lu [q.v.] died, [https://www.geni.com/people/YK/6000000009796754104 I-k'uang] was given the highest official position in the empire. From 1903 to 1911 he served as chief Grand Councilor, a
2 CONC nd from May to November 1911 had the rank of premier. His conduct was such, however, that he was several times openly accused of corruption and of hoarding great wealth. It is reported that whereas other corrupt officials received
2 CONC  bribes through intermediaries, he insisted on personally negotiating every such transaction. So long as Empress Hsiao-ch'in was living he and his strong supporter, Yüan Shih-k'ai (see under Yüan Chia-san), could do virtually as t
2 CONC hey pleased. After her death (1908), however, he could not maintain his power, being opposed by many Imperial Clansmen who themselves were eager to have it. Meanwhile his son, Tsai-chên 載振, gained notoriety in several scandals. Fi
2 CONC nally, in November 1911, [https://www.geni.com/people/YK/6000000009796754104 I-k'uang] was forced, by the rising tide of revolution, to resign and was made president of the Privy Council. A month later the young emperor, P'u-i (se
2 CONC e under Tsai-t'ien), abdicated and [https://www.geni.com/people/YK/6000000009796754104 I-k'uang] went to Tientsin where he died in 1916. P'u-i conferred on him the posthumous name, '''Mi''' 密. 
