1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''HSIAO-hsien Huang-hou''' 孝獻皇后, Empress Hsiao-hsien, 1639–1660, Sept. 23, favorite consort of Emperor Shih-tsu (i.e. Fu-lin, q.v.), was the daughter of Oši 鄂碩 (d. 1657) of the Donggo clan and the Plain White Bann
2 CONC er. Oši took part in many campaigns from 1634 to 1650 and was rewarded with the hereditary rank of viscount, which was raised to a third class earldom in 1656, as a favor to his daughter. His son, Fiyanggû [q.v.], was the famous c
2 CONC ommander who in 1696 defeated Galdan [q.v.] in Mongolia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hsiao-hsien, also known as '''Tung-o Fei''' 董鄂妃 (Imperial Secondary Consort of the Donggo clan), entered the Palace in 1656 at eighteen ''sui''. According to some Jesuit accounts she had been the "wife of a young Tartar Lord”, but
2 CONC  Emperor Shih-tsu passionately loved her and after her husband's death took her to be his consort (see under Fu-lin). At any rate, suddenly in 1656, she became the Emperor's favorite, was given the title of Hsien-fei 賢妃 (Virtuous 
2 CONC Imperial Consort of the Second Class), and a month later was raised to the rank of Huang Kuei-fei 皇貴妃, or Imperial Consort of the First Class, a rank next only to that of Empress. Shih-tsu had deposed his first Empress, but was ba
2 CONC rred from deposing a second with a view to making Hsiao-hsien Empress, owing to the opposition of the Dowager Empress, Hsiao-chuang [q.v.], and the officials. But he lavished his love on her, and gave her more favors than were due
2 CONC  a woman of her rank. Thus late in 1656, or early in 1657, following the ceremonies which made her Huang Kuei-fei, a general amnesty was proclaimed and her father was made an earl. She gave birth to a son on November 12, 1657 who 
2 CONC died on February 25 of the following year. Contrary to practice this child was posthumously made a prince of the first class, with the title Jung Ch'in-wang 榮親王. In the Palace Hsiao-hsien studied Chinese and practiced calligraphy,
2 CONC  and under the influence of the Emperor she also studied Ch'an (Zen) Buddhism. When she died the Emperor was greatly moved, and issued a long account of her life in which he enumerated her virtues. She was posthumously made an Emp
2 CONC ress and was canonized as '''Hsiao-hsien Huang-hou'''. Her body was borne by high officials to the hillock, Ching-shan 景山, north of the palace in Peking, where elaborate Buddhistic ceremonies were conducted at enormous cost and wh
2 CONC ere her remains were cremated. Certain eunuchs and maids in the Palace committed suicide in order that their spirits might accompany her. This practice, known as ''hsün-tsang'' 殉葬, had long been abandoned by Chinese rulers, but wa
2 CONC s retained by the Manchus until this time (see under Empress Hsiao-lieh).
2 CONT
2 CONT After Empress Hsiao-hsien died the Emperor could not control himself for grief, and he himself died four and a half months later. Thereupon the Dowager Empress and the Manchu regents issued an alleged will of the deceased Emperor 
2 CONC in which he declared himself blameworthy for many things, among them the lavish and costly posthumous rites he had accorded the Empress Hsiao-hsien. This, however, may have been only the opinion of the Dowager Empress. Probably in
2 CONC  deference to her wishes, too, Empress Hsiao-hsien was not given the Emperor's posthumous designation, Chang (see under Fu-lin), which was necessary if her name was to be celebrated in the Imperial Ancestral Hall. Despite this dis
2 CONC crimination, her ashes were deposited in the tomb of the Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legends concerning Empress Hsiao-hsien soon grew up. According to one, she was in reality a Chinese woman, named Tung Po (see under Mao Hsiang), popularly known as Tung Hsiao-wan who, owing to her beauty, had been abducted by Manc
2 CONC hu soldiers and sent to the Palace. Stories also arose to the effect that the Emperor was so grief stricken after her death that he became a Buddhist monk, although ceremonies were performed as if he had really died. Some writers 
2 CONC professed to believe that the novel, ''Hung-lou mêng'', or ''Dream of the Red Chamber'' (see under Ts'ao Chan), was based on the love affair of Hsiao-hsien and the Emperor. But enough actual data are now available to prove these s
2 CONC uppositions groundless.
2 CONT
2 CONT [1/220/8a; ''Ch'ing lieh-ch'ao Hou-fei chuan kao'' (see under Su-shun) 上/65a; ''Ch'ing Huang-shih ssŭ-p'u'' (see under Fu-lung-an) 2/9a; Chin Chih-chün [q.v.], 端敬皇后傳 in 松鄰叢書 ''Sung-lin ts'ung-shu''; 胡適文存 ''Hu Shih wên-ts'un'' 3/18
2 CONC 5-248; See bibliography under Fu-lin.]
2 CONT
2 CONT M. Jean Gates
