1 NAME Mingju 端範 /納蘭/
2 GIVN Mingju 端範
2 SURN 納蘭
2 _MARNM [Nara]
1 NAME Mingju 明珠
1 NAME 珠 端範 /納蘭/ 三
2 GIVN 珠 端範
2 SURN 納蘭
2 NSFX 三
2 _MARNM 明
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1635
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1708
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Mingju''' 明珠 (T. 端範), 1635-1708, official, was a grandson of Gintaisi [q.v.] of the Nara clan. Gintaisi was one of the two rulers of the Yehe nation which was conquered by Nurhaci [q.v.] in 1619. [https://www.ge
2 CONC ni.com/people/MZ/6000000009821674495 Mingju] was nine years old when his family, serving under the Manchu Plain Yellow Banner, moved to Peking in 1644 as nobles of the new dynasty. At first an officer of the Imperial Bodyguard, he
2 CONC  rose by degrees to be a sub-chancellor of the Hung-wên yüan 弘文院 in 1666. In 1668 he became president of the Board of Punishments; a year later president of the Censorate; and in 1671 president of the Board of War. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When Wu San-kuei [q.v.], in 1673, pretended to [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor Shêng-tsu] that he was willing for the three frontier garrisons or San-fan of South China (of which the one at Yunnan was u
2 CONC nder his command) to be transferred to Liaotung, most officials in Peking advised the [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor] not to carry out the program for fear that it would lead to civil war. [https://www
2 CONC .geni.com/people/MZ/6000000009821674495 Mingju] and [https://www.geni.com/people/MSH/6000000010103943941 Mishan] [q.v.], however, insisted on accepting the challenge and even resorting to arms if Wu failed to comply. The young [ht
2 CONC tps://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor] approved the suggestion, thus precipitating the San-fan Rebellion which covered ten provinces and lasted eight years. During the war the [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/600
2 CONC 0000019155600599 Emperor] relied much on [https://www.geni.com/people/MZ/6000000009821674495 Mingju], appointing him president of the Civil Office in 1675 and a Grand Secretary in 1677. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/MZ/6000000009821674495 Mingju] was friendly to Chinese scholars and saw to it that his sons were well-versed in Chinese literature. But in politics he followed the example of Oboi and Songgotu [qq.v.] 
2 CONC in permitting the formation of a political group which stooped to bribery and corruption. By appointing his followers to key positions, he became in the sixteen-eighties the most powerful official in the empire. However, early in 
2 CONC 1688, Kuo Hsiu [q.v.], then a censor, memorialized the throne on eight instances of corruption practiced by this group and designated [https://www.geni.com/people/MZ/6000000009821674495 Mingju] as the leader. Having been long awar
2 CONC e of the situation, the [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor] acted at once and punished all who were involved. [https://www.geni.com/people/MZ/6000000009821674495 Mingju] was deprived of his titles and offi
2 CONC ces, but in the same year was made a senior assistant chamberlain of the Imperial Bodyguard, a post he held until his death twenty years later. During the wars against Galdan [q.v.] he served as commissioner of grain transport for
2 CONC  the expeditionary forces. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A skillful business executive, he spent most of his later years in commercial enterprises (see under An Ch'i) and thus accumulated a vast fortune which remained in the family till the close of the eighteenth century when one of hi
2 CONC s descendants, Ch'êng-an 成安, was falsely accused by [https://www.geni.com/people/HS/6000000009797419358 Ho-shên] [q.v.], and the family property was confiscated. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The family of [https://www.geni.com/people/MZ/6000000009821674495 Mingju] was closely related by marriage to the Ch'ing imperial house. His great-aunt was the mother of Abahai [q.v.], and he himself married a daughter of Ajige [q.
2 CONC v.]. The youngest of his three sons, [https://www.geni.com/people/KF/6000000009821874530 K'uei-fang 揆芳], married a woman of the imperial clan. His eldest son, [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde], was a well
2 CONC  known poet and the second son, [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000009821596570 K'uei-hsü] [qq.v.], also a poet, was one of the powerful political figures in the later K'ang-hsi period. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [ 1/275/2b; 3/9/19a; 34/151/4b; ''Pa-ch'i Man-chou shih-tsu t'ung-p'u'' (see under Anfiyanggû), 22/2a; ''Tung-hua-lu'', ''Ch'ien-lung'' 37: 10; Chao-lien [q.v.], ''Hsiao-t'ing hsü-lu'' 5/23a; ''Ku-tung so-chi'' (see bibl. under La
2 CONC ng Ving-chi) 6/19a; Haenisch, E., ''T'oung Pao'', 1913, p. 91; Shêng-yü [q.v.], ''Hsüeh-chi hsün-pei lu'', 13/4a.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT FANG CHAO-YING
