2 DATE 19 JAN 1655
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1685
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Singde''' 性德 (T.容若 H. 楞伽山人), original name, '''Cengde''' 成德, Jan. 19, 1655-1685, July 1, poet and official, was the eldest son of [https://www.geni.com/people/MZ/6000000009821674495 Mingju] [q.v.] of the Yehe Na
2 CONC ra clan, and a member of the Manchu Plain Yellow Banner. When he took his ''chü-jên'' degree, in 1672, one of the chief examiners was [https://www.geni.com/people/XQX/6000000069836370427 Hsü Ch'ien-hsüeh] [q.v.] who later did his 
2 CONC utmost to advance the studies and the fame of this Manchu scholar. Although he passed the metropolitan examination in 1673 at the age of nineteen (''sui''), [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde] was hindered 
2 CONC by illness from proceeding at once with the palace examinations. In the interval of three years before the next examination he jotted down four ''chüan'' of miscellaneous notes on his studies which he entitled 淥水亭雜識 ''Lu-shui t'in
2 CONC g tsa-chih'', after the name of a pavilion in his father's garden near the Shih-ch'a hai 十剎海 in the northern precincts of Peking. This garden later became the property of [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Prince 
2 CONC Ch'êng] (see under Yung-hsing) and still later of [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 Prince Ch'un] (see under I-huan). In the palace examinations of 1676 [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde]
2 CONC  ranked seventh as ''chin-shih'' of the second class. But instead of appointing him to a literary post, [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor Shêng-tsu] made him an officer of the Imperial Bodyguard. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde]'s fame as a writer of ''tz'û'' (poems in irregular metre), and as a scholar, spread rapidly in literary circles of Peking. By 1678 his second collection of ''tz'û'' was e
2 CONC dited and printed by his friends, [https://www.geni.com/people/GZG/6000000075504498995 Ku Chên-kuan 顧貞觀] (T. 華封, H. 梁汾, b. 1637) and Wu Ch'i [q.v.], under the title 飲水詞 ''Yin-shui tz'û''—the first collection, 側帽詞 ''Ts'e-mao tz'û''
2 CONC , having appeared some time earlier. Many of the Chinese scholars who were summoned to Peking to take the special examination in 1679, known as ''po-hsüeh hung-tz'û'' (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/PSY/6000000075506035910
2 CONC  P'êng Sun-yü]), became his intimate friends, and not a few were benefited financially by him or through the influence of his father who had been made a Grand Secretary in 1677. Apparently it was [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/60
2 CONC 00000009821582884 Singde]'s hospitality to Chinese scholars which gave rise to the theory that he was the hero of [https://www.geni.com/people/CZ/6000000013630571684 Ts'ao Chan]'s [q.v.] famous novel, ''Hung-lou mêng''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1682 [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde] joined the commission under Laugtan and Pengcun [qq.v.] which was sent to investigate the activities of Russia in the Amur region. From then on he accompanied the
2 CONC  [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor] on many tours outside the capital—once to Chekiang in 1684. When the [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor] set out in June 1685 on a trip to the 
2 CONC Eastern Tombs, [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde] was too ill to go. He died in the following month, leaving three sons and two daughters. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The collected works of [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde], in 20 ''chüan'', were edited by [https://www.geni.com/people/XQX/6000000069836370427 Hsü Ch'ien-hsüeh] and printed in 1691 under the title ''T'ung
2 CONC -chih t'ang chi'', after the name of his studio. This work received descriptive notice in the ''Ssû-k'u'' Catalog (see under Chi Yün), as did two others on the Classics which seem, however, to have been compiled by Chinese scholar
2 CONC s and later attributed to [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde]. His studio name appears in the title of the great collection of 138 treatises on the Nine Classics, entitled 通志堂集 ''T'ung-chih t'ang ching-chie
2 CONC h'' (經解). [https://www.geni.com/people/XQX/6000000069836370427 Hsü Ch'ien-hsüeh]'s preface to it states that [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde] provided the funds to initiate the printing. His name appears
2 CONC  in the margins as the editor, and there are various perfunctory prefaces attributed to him, but these were probably written by others. The work itself seems not to have been printed until after [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/600
2 CONC 0000009821582884 Singde]'s death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde] ranks as one of the great poets of the Ch'ing period, especially in the writing of ''tz'û'' in which he followed patterns set in the period of the Five Dynasties (907-960
2 CONC ). The tragic and passionate mood of much of his poetry, and his premature death, invite comparison with the T'ang poet, Li Ho 李賀 (790-816), or with [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Keats/6000000030264976703 John Keats]. It is sa
2 CONC id that his poems were inspired by his frustrated love for a cousin who was taken to the Palace and whom he therefore could not marry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde]'s eldest son, [https://www.geni.com/people/FG/6000000010233570398 Fuge 富格] (or 福哥, 1675-1700), was selected to serve [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 E
2 CONC mperor Shêng-tsu] as a page, but died before he could rise to a higher position. [https://www.geni.com/people/FG/6000000010233570398 Fuge] left a son, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZD/6000000010233690631 Jandai 瞻岱] (1700-1741?, pos
2 CONC thumous name 恭勤), who served as provincial commander-in-chief of Chihli (1737-38) and of Kansu (after 1738). [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde]'s second son, [https://www.geni.com/people/FED/60000000101035
2 CONC 88077 Furdon 富爾敦], was a ''chin-shih'' of 1700. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde] is remembered for his part in bringing about the release of the exiled poet, [https://www.geni.com/people/WZQ/6000000080201796833 Wu Chao-ch'ien 吳兆騫] (T. 漢槎, 1631-1684), 
2 CONC a native Wu-chiang, Kiangsu, who became a ''chü-jên'' 1657 at the provincial examination in Nanking. Later in that year one of the chief examiners was accused of corruption, and the successful candidates (also under suspicion for 
2 CONC irregularity) were examined in Peking. Though an able writer, [https://www.geni.com/people/WZQ/6000000080201796833 Wu] was for some reason adjudged unworthy of ''chü-jên'' degree and was exiled to Ninguta in Manchuria. He reached 
2 CONC his destination in 1659 and endured there an exile's life for twenty-two years. Occasionally he corresponded with old friends, among them the above-mentioned Ku Chên-kuan and [https://www.geni.com/people/XQX/6000000069836370427 Hs
2 CONC ü Ch'ien-hsüeh]. In 1676 Ku wrote a poem which he dedicated to his friend in exile. [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde] read it and was so moved that he promised to work for [https://www.geni.com/people/WZQ
2 CONC /6000000080201796833 Wu]'s release—the method used was to make public [https://www.geni.com/people/WZQ/6000000080201796833 Wu]'s merits as a writer. The commission which went to Kirin in 1677 to locate the highest peak of the Ch'a
2 CONC ng-pai shan (see under Sabsu) brought back a long poem by [https://www.geni.com/people/WZQ/6000000080201796833 Wu] eulogizing the mountain as the place where the Ch'ing Imperial Family had originated. This poem was presented to th
2 CONC e throne. Other poems by [https://www.geni.com/people/WZQ/6000000080201796833 Wu] were edited and printed by [https://www.geni.com/people/XQX/6000000069836370427 Hsü Ch'ien-hsüeh] under the title, 秋笳集 ''Ch'iu-chia chi'', 4 ''chüan
2 CONC ''. It was not difficult then for [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde]'s father to persuade the [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor] to release the exile, and in 1681 he was given his
2 CONC  freedom. [https://www.geni.com/people/XD/6000000009821582884 Singde] helped further to rehabilitate [https://www.geni.com/people/WZQ/6000000080201796833 Wu] by engaging him to teach his brother, K'uei-hsü [q.v.], who later studie
2 CONC d under Cha Shên-hsing [q.v.]. The poet's wife had voluntarily joined him in exile in 1661, and while there gave birth to a son, Wu Chên-ch'ên 吳桭臣 (T. 南榮, b. 1664), who in 1726 re-edited his father's works in 8 ''chüan''—likewise 
2 CONC with the above-mentioned title, ''Ch'iu-chia chi''. This son was the author of a descriptive account of Ninguta, entitled 寧古塔紀略 ''Ninguta chi-lüeh'', printed about 1721. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [ ''Nien-p'u'' by Chang Jên-chêng in ''Kuo-hsüeh chi-k'an'' (''Sinological Journal''), vol. 2, no. 4, pp. 741-90; 1/489/26b;3/327/40a; ''Ssû-k'u'', 6/7a,21/5a,183/6a; ''Tung-hua lu'', Ch'ien-lung 101/8a; ''Hu Shih wên-ts'un'' (see
2 CONC  bibl. under Li Ju-chên) 1st series, vol. 3, p. 196; ''Ninguta chi-lüeh''; Shêng-yü [q.v.], ''Hsüeh-chi hsün-pei lu'', 13/3a; Su Hsüeh-lin, "A Study of Two Tz'û Writers of the Ch'ing Period" (in Chinese), ''Quarterly Journal of Li
2 CONC beral Arts'', Wuhan University, vol. 1, no. 3; ''Kao-tsung shih-lu'' (see under Hung-li), 136/13a.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT FANG CHAO-YING
